Wait — should `b` itself (the booking object) be required to be an object? R1 says all bookings require a non-empty string id, etc. If a booking is null or not an object, accessing properties will throw its own TypeError — in fact, `typeof null.id` throws TypeError anyway, so it satisfies the condition of throwing a TypeError. But to be safe, explicitly check for non-object and throw a TypeError with a message. Note: accessing `b.id` when b is a primitive like a string actually works (e.g., `"abc".id` is undefined). But a booking that's a string can't have a valid id, so it'll throw TypeError anyway. No problem.

Order of checks per R3: R1 for that booking (all of id, room, start, end format), then the end check, then duplicate id. My loop does exactly that: verify all fields of R1 first, then range, then duplicate. Good.

Subtle point on ordering within R1: if a booking has an invalid id and a duplicate id — the R1 failure comes first. No problem.

